  {
    "objectID": "index.html#our-mission",
    "href": "index.html#our-mission",
    "title": "SWAPER",
    "section": "Our mission",
    "text": "Our mission\nThe Department of Surveillance, Warning, Preparedness, and Emergency Response is tasked with protecting public health through a structured and systematic approach. The department’s mandate is executed through three primary missions:\n1. To Ensure the Quality of the Public Health Monitoring System\nThis mission focuses on the development and oversight of processes that guarantee the accuracy, completeness, and timeliness of data from all public health surveillance systems. It includes conducting systematic evaluations and rapid surveys to inform and continuously improve monitoring strategies.\n2. To Monitor, Detect, and Alert on Unusual Health Events\nThis mission involves the systematic collection and management of epidemiological data to monitor health trends, forecast outcomes, and assess risks. The objective is to promptly detect unusual events, issue evidence-based alerts, and propose scientifically grounded solutions to mitigate public health threats.\n3. To Prepare for and Respond to Public Health Emergencies\nThis mission centers on maintaining a high state of operational readiness. It includes managing the Public Health Emergency Operations Center (PHEOC) and the Provincial Rapid Response Teams (RRT), and implementing comprehensive preparedness activities such as planning, training, drills, and resource allocation for effective emergency response."
  },
  {
    "objectID": "index.html#our-work",
    "href": "index.html#our-work",
    "title": "SWAPER",
    "section": "Our work",
    "text": "Our work\nTo accomplish these missions, the department is organized into two primary divisions with distinct functions:\nDivision of Surveillance and Warning\nThis division is responsible for all data collection, analysis, and risk assessment functions. Its key responsibilities include:\n\nAssessing the performance and quality of existing surveillance systems.\nDeveloping and implementing an integrated, digital disease surveillance system, including an early warning mechanism for schools and communities, with a target for completion by 2025.\nConducting formal risk assessments for endemic, zoonotic, and emerging/re-emerging infectious diseases.\nEstablishing official epidemic warning thresholds for all administrative levels (city and ward).\nDeveloping and applying mathematical models for the purpose of forecasting disease outbreaks.\nDefining and utilizing key data indicators for the evaluation of outbreak severity and response effectiveness.\n\nDivision of Preparedness and Emergency Response\nThis division is responsible for all operational planning, readiness, and response coordination. Its key responsibilities include:\n\nAdvising on and developing the legal and operational frameworks for the Public Health Emergency Operations Center (PHEOC) and the Rapid Response Teams (RRT).\nDeveloping and standardizing operational guidelines and Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for the PHEOC and RRTs.\nCreating and managing the activation system for the Rapid Response Teams.\nOverseeing the strategic planning, procurement, and maintenance of essential infrastructure, equipment, and supplies for emergency response.\nProviding technical guidance on the development of the city’s long-term Pandemic Response Plan for 2030 and beyond."
  },
  {
    "objectID": "index.html#collaboration",
    "href": "index.html#collaboration",
    "title": "SWAPER",
    "section": "Collaboration",
    "text": "Collaboration\nWe maintain strong working relationships with leading academic research institutions, non-governmental organizations (NGOs), and other specialized departments within the Ho Chi Minh City Center for Disease Control (HCDC).\nThese partnerships are vital for conducting cutting-edge research, validating new methodologies, and strengthening the city’s overall public health framework. Notable partners include the Oxford University Clinical Research Unit (OUCRU) and FHI 360.\nA selection of key research projects undertaken through these collaborations includes:\n\nEvaluation of the Reproduction Number (Rt) in Measles Outbreaks in HCMC.\nMethods for Determining Epidemic Thresholds in Endemic Disease Surveillance: A Scoping Review and Benchmark.\nDengue Advanced Readiness Tools (DART).\nAssessment of the Infectious Disease and Public Health Event Surveillance System in HCMC.\nA Review of Methodological Approaches to Infectious Disease Risk Assessment.\nEvent-Based Surveillance Using Social Media, Google Search Data, and Online News Media."
  },
  {
    "objectID": "news/post1.html",
    "href": "news/post1.html",
    "title": "Meeting with OUCRU",
    "section": "",
    "text": "On July 02, 2025, the SWAPER development team held a formal meeting with representatives from the Oxford University Clinical Research Unit (OUCRU) in Ho Chi Minh City. This was one of the initial steps in building a long-term partnership between the two organizations, centered around infectious disease surveillance and applied epidemiological research.\nThe meeting provided an opportunity for both sides to discuss key challenges in early warning systems, explore local data collection needs at the district level, and share perspectives on the use of artificial intelligence in outbreak detection and prediction. OUCRU shared their experience with previous research projects, while SWAPER presented their ongoing efforts to digitize surveillance workflows and develop integrated platforms for real-time monitoring.\nThe atmosphere of the meeting was open and collaborative, fostering mutual understanding and laying the groundwork for future joint initiatives. As of July 2025, the discussions from this meeting have helped shape several shared projects and contributed to a growing network of partners committed to improving public health systems in Vietnam."
  },
